).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707fbf1b-bf7d-5995-b7cc-be64436a3be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389541} {:answer/id #uuid "d22e14ca-11b5-5af0-a855-e0dc3f5271d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389542} {:answer/id #uuid "1adc692e-fe37-5019-893d-bd451359ba0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389543} {:answer/id #uuid "c552ac7b-ee72-589e-9065-cf0a29c3f734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389544} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6ee24c-0ae3-5eec-973f-6e3492e4e1fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d22e14ca-11b5-5af0-a855-e0dc3f5271d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389542} :answer-field/id #uuid "bea9a704-6834-5c1c-808b-11ae8ac08e51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389540}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aefa1f59-5dce-5a67-8312-bce584cb45c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-164522" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse \n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36\\,⟹\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1\n$$\n\nin $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{36} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ed22918-85fb-5c79-9620-31cecfbbc27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389555} {:answer/id #uuid "d4533c8f-a193-52ed-a9d9-fc9ccfbdbf2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389556} {:answer/id #uuid "974a57ae-291f-5842-95b3-64380db1d779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389557} {:answer/id #uuid "814a367c-72f0-5937-973d-1f16f3d1e3d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389558} {:answer/id #uuid "487b991c-c37d-5069-8337-bf2040028617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4533c8f-a193-52ed-a9d9-fc9ccfbdbf2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389556} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d86410-beeb-5cce-9a2f-324bd43cac89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389554}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353926d7-638c-5e50-a022-4791f5ce8a3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164524" :question/problem "$$\nf(t,z)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(t,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ in $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{cos}^{2}⁡t}{25}+\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡t}{4} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing an Elliptic Cylinder"}]}
